---
import InstrumentBand from "../../../components/lab/instrument/InstrumentBand.astro";
import AtmosphereFlight from "../../../components/studies/rock-atmospheres/AtmosphereFlight.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (item) => item.slug === "rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15",
);
---

<StudyLayout {study}>
  <section>
    <h2>Accepted / Placeholder Earth for Practice</h2>
    <p>
      16 November 2026. Impact with the accepted Cirrus atmosphere is selected
      as placeholder Earth in Practice. The atmosphere and ship-entry treatment
      are accepted; Earth and inhabited-world exploration concludes for now.
      This is a playable placeholder, not an accepted Earth geography or
      habitation design.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use Cirrus, 91% lit-cloud brightness, the original 02.11 shadow (42%),
      shared sunlight, 1.45× rock shadow and the fixed blue Dayward rim, peaking
      20° toward the Sun. Keep opaque clouds with openings, the current
      atmosphere extent, and the accepted soft planet-facing LFX glow. Heat FX
      uses the curved band at 180% brightness and 150% width, stronger toward
      the planet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Accepted production behavior: 1 hull/s in the outermost 30% of the
      atmosphere and 2 hull/s deeper inside, plus a small deceleration opposite
      current travel. This slows the ship without steering it. The drag
      coefficient remains implementation tuning; the study’s 0.8/s fixture is
      not a production value. No accumulated-temperature or delayed-damage model
      is required. This behavior is not currently installed in Practice.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-16-accepted-heat.png"
        alt="Accepted curved-band heat around a Wasp skimming the Impact atmosphere"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Accepted visual settings, captured 16 November: B1, Skim, 0.45s, Follow
        4×. Impact/Cirrus, 180% band brightness, 150% band width, soft LFX
        contact glow. This visual fixture does not apply atmospheric damage.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-16-accepted-heat-flight.png"
        alt="The accepted atmospheric heat cue at Follow flight scale"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <figcaption>
        The same settings and instant at Follow scale. Stills retain the
        appearance; the instrument below retains the entry/exit motion
        reference.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The Flight instrument remains as the integration reference for comparing a
      future production adoption against accepted appearance, entry and fading
      exit at identical burns and times. Its earlier options are diagnostic
      references, not reopened decisions. The production adoption was removed
      after its shared-rendering changes caused regressions; Practice remains on
      its prior rendering and simulation paths.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="01">01 / Air the pilot can read</h2>
    <p>
      A close pass should reveal air before the ship enters it. The envelope
      needs enough presence to make entry foreseeable, while leaving the rock's
      mineral regions, worn terrain and turning limb legible. Its consequence
      should give the pilot a small, understandable flying choice. Pleasurable
      flight, responsive feedback and convincing material guide this balance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use
      <a href="/lab/studies/mercury-2026-11-12#02-19"
        >Mercury 02.19's three authored Lab exemplars</a
      >, selected for this cycle—Mineral mosaic, Ancient highlands and Impact
      palimpsest—with their spherical mapping, independent 31.3-second turns and
      Sun-facing light. Begin at current Practice Venus's approximately 40.33u
      body radius, beside the real fitted Wasp at flight scale. Size is a
      comparison condition, not an Earth-like identity. Existing gravity,
      controls and solid-body contact supply the flight baseline; the first
      fixture borrows Practice Venus's orbit and gravity without retuning
      either.
    </p>
    <p>
      Production Venus has a visual atmosphere without drag or atmospheric
      damage. Appearance and entry response remain open together. A shallow drag
      layer is the first hypothesis: stay outside to preserve speed, or skim
      through air to shed it. Its proposed 10u radial depth is in world units,
      not typographic points or damage points; thickness, colour and drag
      strength are provisional. Danger is a possibility, not a required damage
      rule. Surface redesign, Earth-like geography, habitation and a planet
      taxonomy are outside this question. Venus's body/envelope separation and
      return to whole-planet views inform the method without prescribing its
      appearance.
    </p>
    <p class="question-test">
      Question test · 15 November 2026: One subject — Pass; Grounded — Pass with
      findings; Consequence — Pass. The opening now distinguishes Lab exemplars
      from production adoption and the proposed mechanics from Venus's visual
      atmosphere. Not rerun after correction.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15">Full review</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="02">02 / Skim the air</h2>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-1">
        02.1 / A visible envelope with a small braking consequence
      </summary>
      <p>
        The first proposal adds a pale mineral-blue column of air: faint over
        the face, stronger at the limb, falling away into space. Its light
        follows the Sun while the original rock turns beneath it. Colour,
        opacity, depth and drag are experimental values, not selected
        properties.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Successful, usable step · 15 November 2026.</strong>
        The Flight scene instrument is the right setting for this investigation.
        Its standard game look and feel, real ship and predictable burn
        scenarios give a useful feel for how an atmospheric encounter could work
        in the game. The shared timeline makes approach, entry and exit
        inspectable and repeatable.
      </p>
      <p>
        This accepts the usefulness of the example and the instrument as the
        working method. The pale envelope and drag are a usable starting point;
        their exact appearance, thickness and strength are still provisional.
        The planet's existing shadow remains a working value, not a settled
        lighting decision.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-1-flight.png"
          alt="02.1 mineral rock and Wasp at flight scale in the Flight scene instrument"
          width="1280"
          height="1000"
          loading="lazy"
        >
        <figcaption>
          Retained 02.1 frame: Mineral mosaic, air + drag, B1 at 0.5s, Follow
          0.8, original shadow, 1280 × 1000 browser. Captured before the 02.2
          controls.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <details>
        <summary>02.1 / Enlarged construction reference</summary>
        <img
          src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-1-close.png"
          alt="The same 02.1 entry at Follow 4× with the original shadow"
          width="1280"
          height="1000"
          loading="lazy"
        >
        <p>
          Same material, burn and instant at Follow 4×. These stills retain the
          visual reference; they do not reproduce the motion or establish
          gameplay feel.
        </p>
      </details>
      <p>
        At 10u depth, B1 and B2 enter at approximately 0.37s and leave by 0.55s;
        B3 leaves by 0.58s. The opening at 0.5s is inside the air. All three
        skim without solid contact over the full 14-second sequence. These are
        measured fixture conditions, not a judgment of interaction quality.
      </p>
      <p>
        The envelope extends 10u beyond the solid radius of 40.33u. Entry begins
        when the ship's collision circle overlaps that shell; density increases
        linearly with penetration to the surface. Each 1/60-second step
        multiplies planet-relative velocity by exp(−0.8 × density × dt). This is
        a fixture approximation of drag, not a new production physics law. Air
        has the planet's orbital velocity; winds and atmospheric co-rotation are
        omitted. There is no atmospheric damage. Solid contact retains the
        game's bounce and hull cost.
      </p>
      <p>
        Conditions: production Practice Wasp with starter fitting, current Venus
        radius, orbit, travel speed and gravity; one planet at orbital angle
        zero, with the production Sun retained. Other planets are omitted. The
        ship starts 82u left and 100u above the planet, heading down, with
        downward speed 230u/s plus the planet's orbital speed. B1 opens at 0.5
        seconds. The original materials retain Mercury 02.19's mirrored
        spherical mapping and independent 31.3-second turn. Follow uses 0.8
        render px/u before the instrument's display scaling; Follow 4× uses 4
        render px/u before display scaling.
      </p>
      <p>
        The shared Flight scene instrument now owns planet staging and
        repeatable atmospheric drag. The study supplies only material/envelope
        painters and pane options. Its production ship, fixed burns, timeline,
        views and layer composition remain the instrument's. Study flight never
        uses manual piloting.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-2">02.2 / Shadow depth beneath the atmosphere</summary>
      <p>
        Impact palimpsest is the default reference for this round. With air
        around the body, the current shadow feels light. Varying shadow
        intensity tests whether stronger depth changes the feel of the planet
        and its initial atmospheric treatment without changing the surface,
        envelope or flight.
      </p>
      <p>
        The burger pane now includes Planet shadow intensity. 1× exactly
        restores the 02.1 lighting; 0× removes its shadow darkening, and 2×
        doubles it. Only the darkening side of the Sun-facing light mask
        changes. Sunlit highlights, light direction, material rotation and
        atmospheric drawing remain fixed. Because the body's light pass also
        shades the air over its face, that overlap changes with the shadow; the
        outer envelope keeps its original drawing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Finding · 15 November 2026:</strong>
        shadow intensity can reach 1.45× without making bare planets feel
        plastic or rendered. The same strength also works with the initial
        atmosphere. This establishes a usable range up to 1.45×, not a universal
        final lighting value.
      </p>
      <p>
        Carry 1.45× into the next comparison as its default, with Impact
        palimpsest retained as the reference material. The slider remains
        available. The atmosphere's initial appearance remains provisional.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary>02.2 / Retained 1.45× reference</summary>
        <img
          src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-2-shadow-145-reference.png"
          alt="Impact with the initial limb haze and 1.45× shadow beside the Wasp"
          width="1280"
          height="1000"
          loading="lazy"
        >
        <p>
          Impact, initial limb haze, 1.45× shadow, B1 at 0.5s, Follow 4×, 1280 ×
          1000 browser. Reconstructed when opening 02.3 to retain the comparison
          conditions; this is not a capture of the earlier assessment.
        </p>
      </details>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-3">
        02.3 / Three ways to make the rock feel surrounded by air
      </summary>
      <p>
        Compare three atmospheric treatments in the same flight scenario. Limb
        haze retains the initial silhouette-focused treatment. Thin veil carries
        more air across the face, softening terrain contrast toward the limb.
        Layered air combines a denser low layer with a restrained outer haze,
        testing whether visible depth makes entry easier to anticipate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Impact and 1.45× shadow are the starting conditions. Colour, 10u radial
        interaction depth, drag, airflow feedback, rotation and B1–B3 burns stay
        fixed across the treatments. Their differences are visual; none changes
        where entry occurs or what happens to the ship. The appearance selector
        is in the burger pane. No treatment is selected as the outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        Judge approach, entry and exit at flight scale: whether the rock retains
        its material character, the envelope reads as air, and entry is
        foreseeable. Use Follow 4× to inspect construction, then return to
        Follow for the whole planet beside the ship.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Findings · 16 November 2026:</strong>
        this is a useful comparison that advances the atmosphere design. A
        stronger shadow, perhaps 1.75×, may still work, but 1.45× remains the
        agreed baseline. That possibility is not a new selected value.
      </p>
      <p>
        Atmospheric entry is hard to identify when the air has no clearly
        visible boundary. A thin upper layer is a promising next investigation,
        not an accepted border treatment. Its appearance should help anticipate
        the actual interaction boundary.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gas without clouds feels very thin. It has an appealing appearance, but
        may not provide the atmospheric presence this game needs. Cloud-bearing
        alternatives are worth comparing while preserving the existing rock's
        character; neither clouds nor a particular cloud treatment is selected.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-3-limb-haze-flight-reconstruction.png"
          alt="Limb haze at flight scale beside the Wasp"
          width="1280"
          height="1000"
          loading="lazy"
        >
        <figcaption>
          02.3 reconstruction · 16 November 2026, before opening 02.4. Impact,
          Limb haze, shadow 1.45×, B1 at 0.5s, Follow; 1280 × 1000 browser. This
          retains the baseline conditions, not the earlier observation itself.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <details>
        <summary>02.3 / Reconstructed treatment comparison</summary>
        {[
          ["limb-haze", "Limb haze"],
          ["thin-veil", "Thin veil"],
          ["layered-air", "Layered air"],
        ].map(([slug, name]) => (
          <figure>
            <img
              src={`/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-3-${slug}-flight-reconstruction.png`}
              alt={`${name} at Follow flight scale`}
              width="1280"
              height="1000"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <img
              src={`/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-3-${slug}-close-reconstruction.png`}
              alt={`${name} enlarged at Follow 4×`}
              width="1280"
              height="1000"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>
              {name}: Follow and Follow 4×, same B1 opening and material.
              Reconstructed 16 November 2026. Stills preserve appearance; they
              do not establish the earlier motion judgment.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </details>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-4">
        02.4 / An upper extent and something within it
      </summary>
      <p>
        A visible upper layer could make the approaching edge easier to
        anticipate. Clouds could give the volume substance while openings keep
        the authored rock visible. These are separate hypotheses: a cloudless
        upper layer isolates the first, and two kinds of cloud cover test the
        second against that same edge.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Limb haze:</strong>
          the unchanged 02.3 starting reference.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Upper layer:</strong>
          a thin, softly bounded shell just inside the 10u extent, with the
          original low haze beneath it. Tests whether a visible edge reads as
          air or becomes too much like a ring.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloud banks:</strong>
          broken pale masses below that shell, leaving openings onto the rock.
          Tests substance through local overlap.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloud bands:</strong>
          broader, winding coverage beneath the same shell. Tests whether
          continuity gives the air more presence or conceals too much terrain.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Impact, 1.45× shadow, the original 31.3s rock turn, 10u depth, 0.8/s
        drag and B1–B3 remain fixed. Cloud cover is a procedural translucent
        overlay at 4.2u altitude with a provisional 38s turn; it does not
        replace the rock material or change flight. Its pattern follows
        simulation time through pause and seek. All three original materials and
        the shadow slider remain available. No appearance is selected as the
        outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compare the approach at 0.30s, entry at about 0.37s, the 0.50s skim and
        exit by 0.60s in Follow beside the ship. Entry is collision-circle
        overlap, so the hull reaches the air before the ship centre crosses the
        visible edge. Follow 4× explains the shell and cloud construction;
        return to Follow to assess their presence in flight. Stills cannot
        settle encounter feel.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong
          >Round closed · 16 November 2026. Very good, useful
          comparison.</strong
        >
        A visible atmospheric edge is cleaner than haze alone and is the
        direction to explore further. The current thin line is still naive and
        its appearance is not accepted. Air must also read over the planet’s
        face; an envelope visible only outside the rock feels disconnected from
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stronger rock shadows beneath illuminated air remain a possibility, not
        a selected lighting value. Light reaching the atmosphere and clouds may
        explain the missing presence before any shadow increase is needed. 1.45×
        remains the baseline. Neither cloud pattern is selected.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-4-designer-observation.png"
          alt="Designer’s 02.4 observation: pale cloud cover and a thin upper edge around the rock beside the Wasp"
          width="1260"
          height="900"
          loading="lazy"
        >
        <figcaption>
          Designer-supplied 02.4 observation, 16 November 2026. The crop shows
          the edge and cloud-covered face. The controls are outside the crop;
          exact material, shadow, burn, instant and view are unverified. It is
          not the controlled comparison below.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <details>
        <summary>02.4 / Retained controlled comparison</summary>
        {["Limb haze", "Upper layer", "Cloud banks", "Cloud bands"].map(
          (name) => (
            <figure>
              <img
                src={`/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-4-${name.replaceAll(" ", "-")}-flight-reconstruction.png`}
                alt={`${name}, Follow flight scale`}
                width="1280"
                height="1000"
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <img
                src={`/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-4-${name.replaceAll(" ", "-")}-close-reconstruction.png`}
                alt={`${name}, Follow 4× construction view`}
                width="1280"
                height="1000"
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <figcaption>
                {name}, Impact, shadow 1.45×, B1 at 0.50s; Follow then Follow
                4×. Reconstructed 16 November 2026 before implementing 02.5, at
                1280 × 1000. These stills record conditions, not the earlier
                assessment or motion.
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          ),
        )}
      </details>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-5">
        02.5 / Light the air without lifting the rock’s shadow
      </summary>
      <p>
        The previous composition shades clouds, then applies the rock’s shadow
        over both terrain and air across the face. That may suppress the
        separate atmospheric layer. Isolate that overlap before increasing
        shadow strength: compare the same cloud banks, upper edge and 1.45× rock
        shadow with three treatments of Sun light.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>02.4 light:</strong>
          the unchanged reference, with the rock’s light mask also affecting air
          and clouds over its disc.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Separate air:</strong>
          apply the same light mask to rock alone; retain the existing
          Sun-facing colour of air and clouds above it. This tests whether the
          lighting overlap accounts for the missing layer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Face scatter:</strong>
          the same separation plus a restrained projected column of sunlit air
          across the face. This tests whether visible air between the pilot and
          terrain is also needed.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Cloud banks are the diagnostic starting example, not an accepted
        pattern. Upper layer removes clouds for the same comparison; all 02.4
        appearances remain available in this single instrument. Hold the
        material and shadow fixed while switching light at the same burn and
        instant. The original rock materials, rotation, cloud pattern and turn,
        boundary, depth and drag remain unchanged. Follow judges entry beside
        the ship; Follow 4× explains the face, terminator and edge before
        returning to flight scale.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a study-local Sun-light composition using the shared rock
        painter, not a production lighting change or a physically based
        scattering model. In the two proposals the rock’s existing hard-light
        mask is composed into its own material before the air is drawn, instead
        of darkening the combined image from LFX. Nearby engine and weapon light
        reception remains open; this round does not simulate it. Stronger rock
        shadows and a more natural upper-edge treatment remain subsequent
        questions once face visibility is understood.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Finding carried into discussion · 16 November 2026:</strong>
        light cloud banks work substantially better at game scale, but the
        clouds remain difficult to distinguish from terrain across the face. No
        one lighting option was identified as accepted. The comparison isolates
        a lighting overlap, not the adequacy of the cloud material itself.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary>02.5 / Retained light comparison</summary>
        {["02.4 light", "Separate air", "Face scatter"].map((name) => (
          <figure>
            <img
              src={`/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-5-${name.replaceAll(" ", "-")}-flight-reconstruction.png`}
              alt={`${name}, Cloud banks at Follow flight scale`}
              width="1280"
              height="1000"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <img
              src={`/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-5-${name.replaceAll(" ", "-")}-close-reconstruction.png`}
              alt={`${name}, Cloud banks at Follow 4×`}
              width="1280"
              height="1000"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>
              Impact, Cloud banks, 1.45× shadow, B1 at 0.50s, Follow then Follow
              4×. Reconstructed 16 November 2026 before 02.7; these captures
              retain conditions, not the earlier observation itself.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </details>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-6">
        02.6 / Preserve the rock through openings in the clouds
      </summary>
      <p>
        <strong>Design discussion · 16 November 2026.</strong>
        The scope remains the smallest useful combined problem: a playable
        atmosphere that feels convincing around the existing rock. A readable
        encounter and a convincing world must coexist. The body is deliberately
        small and the air deep enough to fly through; photographic proportions
        would avoid that gameplay requirement rather than solve it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The authored rock materials are the fixed quality reference. Their
        appearance is not reopened. Clouds must be able to hide terrain locally;
        preserving the rock’s identity means leaving sufficient openings, not
        making every cloud transparent. Thin air can transmit terrain, while
        substantial cloud masses can obscure it completely. Coverage and opacity
        are independent: sparse clouds can be opaque, and broad cover can be
        thin.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Variable</th>
            <th>Meaning in this study</th>
            <th>Decision</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Coverage</td>
            <td>Where clouds occur and where rock is exposed</td>
            <td>Use openings to preserve rock identity</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Opacity</td>
            <td>How much terrain is visible through a cloud</td>
            <td>Allow fully obscuring cloud cores</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cloud structure</td>
            <td>Masses, edges, curls and internal detail</td>
            <td>Current procedural pattern is provisional</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Light and shadow</td>
            <td>How rock, clouds and gas respond together</td>
            <td>Distinct materials need distinguishable responses</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The supplied satellite reference helps explain material separation:
        recognisable cloud masses and openings distinguish cloud from ground. It
        does not prescribe white clouds, Earth geography or its lighting. In the
        study image, pale cloud and pale rock merge across the face; cloud
        becomes easiest to identify beyond the rock’s silhouette. Lighting
        improvements alone cannot establish whether the provisional cloud shapes
        are adequate.
      </p>
      <p>
        A small library of composed cloud textures is a plausible future means
        of obtaining crisp, intentional structures. Neither its production
        method nor its size is selected. A single convincing reference could be
        enough to judge light before building a library. Fully cloud-obscured
        worlds are a possible extension, not a new requirement for this
        rock-preserving comparison. Surface replacement and planet taxonomy
        remain outside scope.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rock, clouds and gas each have colour, texture and light/shadow
        behaviour; their combination must remain coherent under a shared Sun.
        Future changes in Sun colour and atmosphere colour will need examination
        after a stable example exists. World and LFX are rendering tools, not
        physical material categories: where light and shadow are composed should
        serve the desired image. Nearby engine and weapon illumination,
        cloud-cast shadows and a refined atmospheric edge remain unresolved.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next comparison fixes the existing material, cloud pattern, gas,
        lighting and flight, and separates cloud coverage from opacity. This can
        establish whether local concealment with clear openings improves the
        material reading before a new texture changes the shapes as well. It
        cannot select a final cloud material or lighting architecture.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-7">02.7 / Opaque clouds with room for the rock</summary>
      <p>
        Compare Sparse / Thin against Sparse / Opaque to isolate transmission
        through the same cloud footprint. Then compare Sparse / Opaque against
        Broad / Opaque to isolate the amount of exposed terrain. Broad / Thin
        completes the comparison. Both coverage proposals use the same
        underlying cloud field and edge transition; only its coverage threshold
        changes. Opaque cores fully conceal terrain, while cloud edges retain a
        soft transition.
      </p>
      <p>
        The opening is Sparse / Opaque. Thin caps opacity at 62%; Opaque reaches
        100% inside its cores. These describe optical transmission, not a
        simulated cloud thickness. Sparse and Broad name relative coverage, not
        measured percentages of the globe. The 02.5 footprint with Thin retains
        the earlier mask as a reference under the same Separate air lighting.
      </p>
      <p>
        Impact and shadow 1.45× remain the starting reference. Cloud banks,
        Separate air, the pale cloud colour, 38s cloud turn, original 31.3s rock
        turn, upper edge, 10u air depth and 0.8/s drag stay fixed across the
        four comparisons. Separate air is an experimental condition, not an
        accepted outcome of 02.5. The material and shadow controls remain
        available for secondary checks; keep them fixed for the primary
        comparison.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use Follow at approach (0.30s), entry (about 0.37s), skim (0.50s) and
        exit (0.60s). Inspect cloud cores and openings in Follow 4×, then return
        to Follow beside the Wasp. This round tests concealment and exposed
        rock; the procedural shapes and the naive upper line remain provisional.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Accepted quality · 16 November 2026:</strong>
        opaque cloud masses with openings are a major improvement. The cloud
