        <summary>02.14 / Corrected spherical shading reference</summary>
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-14-bright-face-reference.png"
            alt="Bright white clouds after the spherical shading correction"
            loading="lazy"
          >
          <figcaption>
            Captured during 02.14: 40% coverage map, Bright face at full
            brightness, then-current 24% dark side, shared sunlight 5800 K,
            Impact, 02.5 / Opaque, Separate air, rock shadow 1.45×, Dayward rim,
            B1 0.50s, Follow 4×.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </details>
    </details>
    <h3 id="02-15">02.15 / Set the prominence of white clouds</h3>
    <p>
      Keep the corrected spherical shading and white cloud colour. Restore the
      accepted 42% dark-side setting, then adjust only the lit-cloud brightness
      from the 100% reference down toward 40%. The slider scales lit material
      RGB and blends into the unchanged dark side; it does not thin the clouds
      or change their openings. Shared sunlight still adds highlights in LFX.
      The original 02.13 shading remains available, but this new control applies
      to Bright face only.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with the 40% coverage map at B1 0.50s in Follow. Lower brightness
      until clouds sit comfortably beside the ship and rock while remaining
      clearly above the surface. Check 20% and 80% cover at the same value, then
      inspect Follow 4×. This tests whether brightness accounts for the chunky
      impression; it does not assume the cloud shapes are resolved. The decision
      below settles the appearance reference. Rotation-speed exploration stays
      out of scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Appearance accepted · 16 November 2026:</strong>
      Cirrus is the preferred default with Bright face at 91%, 02.11 cloud
      shadow at 42%, shared sunlight, rock shadow 1.45× and the Dayward blue
      rim. The 20% and 40% coverage maps and Cumulus also work. This appearance
      works across Mineral, Highlands and Impact. The scene retains 02.5
      footprint / Opaque and Separate air as its comparison conditions. Mixed
      weather is ruled out as an offered material: one recognizable cloud form
      is more readable for this illustrative game treatment. No broader planet
      taxonomy is settled.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15/02-15-accepted-cirrus.png"
        alt="Accepted Cirrus atmosphere with 91 percent lit-cloud brightness"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Reconstructed 16 November 2026 from the accepted settings: Impact,
        Cirrus, 91% lit brightness, 42% night brightness, shared sunlight 5800
        K, 1.45× rock shadow, Bright face, 02.5 / Opaque, Separate air, Dayward
        blue rim. Wasp, B1 0.50s, Follow 4×. The existing fixture is
        deterministic, with no random seed; rock and clouds rotate with
        simulation time.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong>Next question:</strong>
      how should the ship’s passage through the atmosphere look against this
      accepted world? A shallow coast or skim would isolate the transition from
      outside to inside, sustained near-edge travel, and exit. Compare boundary
      readability, ship visibility and airflow cues at flight scale. Stage that
      encounter before adding new effects; keep the accepted appearance and
      provisional mechanics fixed. The existing fast crossing remains below as a
      reference, not evidence for a sustained skim. Rotation-speed exploration
      stays out of scope.
    </p>
    <h3 id="02-16">02.16 / Make atmospheric entry feel hot</h3>
    <p>
      Proposed emphasis · 16 November 2026: entry should read as a shallow heat
      hazard; drag is secondary. The accepted Cirrus atmosphere stays fixed.
      This example tests a warm leading-edge envelope and trailing flame as the
      warning cue, before deciding heat damage or a temperature model. Compare
      <strong>None</strong>, the earlier <strong>Airflow</strong> arcs and
      <strong>Heat envelope</strong>
      at the same burn and instant.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Accepted · 16 November:</strong>
      soft glow on live LFX, on the planet-facing side of the ship. Stronger
      flame emission on that side also works. These stay fixed while the FX mark
      is explored. The tapered crescent remains an earlier reference in the
      pane; it is not the selected contact light.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Current question:</strong>
      what should we stamp into FX to make atmospheric burning? The
      <a href="/lab/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11#luminous">plasma study</a>
      showed that a source mark and its accumulated result are different things.
      Borrowing plasma’s luminous blobs made this effect look like plasma and
      was rejected. Here the pane shows the actual source mark before the shared
      layer adds filtering, overlap and fading.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compare <strong>Current envelope</strong>, <strong>Soft patch</strong>,
      <strong>Tapered streak</strong>
      and <strong>Curved band</strong> at the same instant. The current source
      has four animation frames; the three new marks are static. Each mark is
      mirrored around the direction of travel, weighted toward the planet, and
      stamped at the same rate with the same colour range and baseline emission
      gain. Their areas differ, so total emitted light is not equal. The curved
      band is the preferred shape, but its skim cue was too faint. It now opens
      at a proposed 180% brightness and 150% width. Two sliders separate those
      adjustments; 100% on both restores the previous band. Width thickens the
      band at the same radius, and brightness lifts faint emission up to its
      existing cap. LFX, colour, sidedness and encounter mechanics stay fixed.
      180% brightness and 150% width were subsequently accepted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fresh light follows planet-relative velocity and grows with overlap and
      speed. The shared FX surface owns its history and fade after exit; there
      are no separately aged trail marks. LFX remains the accepted soft contact
      glow. This is a visual excitation proxy, not temperature, accumulated dose
      or hull damage. Judge the result in flight, using the source preview to
      understand what went into it.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Approach</th>
          <th>Measured B1–B3 encounter</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Outside</td>
          <td>No atmospheric overlap</td>
          <td>No heat emission; baseline flight</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Skim</td>
          <td>Entry 0.38–0.40s; exit 0.52–0.55s; peak density 0.22–0.33</td>
          <td>Brief, shallow warning</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Deeper</td>
          <td>Entry 0.35s; exit 0.57–0.62s; peak density 0.70–0.81</td>
          <td>Stronger exposure, still escaping the air</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>02.15 pass</td>
          <td>Previous appearance-study trajectory</td>
          <td>Retained reference</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The scene opens at 0.45s in Skim. Inspect approach at 0.30s, peak entry
      around 0.45s, then exit and fading light through 1.2s. Use Follow for
      flight scale and Follow 4× for the envelope’s construction. The ship’s
      initial clearance changes by only a few world units between cases;
      atmosphere depth remains 10u and drag remains 0.8/s. Each case was run
      through all 840 ticks of all three burns without solid contact; hull stays
      at 100. No atmospheric damage is applied. B1/B2 engines fire during entry,
      so this is a grazing-entry comparison, not a sustained engine-off coast.
    </p>
    <p>
      Approach and effect changes preserve the selected burn/time and rebuild
      the shared replay. All atmosphere tools remain available below. Different
      cloud/rock rotation speeds remain out of scope. The unresolved question is
      whether this envelope communicates “I am burning; get out” clearly enough
      to support a later heat-damage experiment. The soft LFX glow and
      planet-facing emphasis are accepted; the curved band at 180% brightness
      and 150% width is accepted.
    </p>
    <p>
      This dated heat-envelope comparison retains the historical Canvas
      starfield behind the light.
    </p>
